From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:16:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: keep the AP frequency when a background CAC starts

A successful CAC start stores the CAC frequency as the frequency of
the BSS. A background CAC runs on a separate chain, but it also
replaced that frequency. Frames that hostapd sends without a
frequency, for example authentication replies, then used the CAC
channel. The kernel refused them as off channel until the next beacon
update stored the operating frequency again.

A background CAC now leaves the stored frequency unchanged. A CAC on
the operating chain still sets it. The AP starts on that channel, and
the kernel events of that CAC find the BSS by this frequency. Events
of the background chain now reach every 5 GHz BSS of the radio, as
they did before after any beacon update.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c |    4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c
+++ b/src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c
@@ -11660,7 +11660,9 @@ static int nl80211_start_radar_detection
 
 	ret = send_and_recv_cmd(drv, msg);
 	if (ret == 0) {
-		nl80211_link_set_freq(bss, freq->link_id, freq->freq);
+		/* a background CAC leaves the AP on its own channel */
+		if (!freq->radar_background)
+			nl80211_link_set_freq(bss, freq->link_id, freq->freq);
 		return 0;
 	}
 
